// D1 round 3 / F2 — the OVER-REFUSAL half of the same site, and the one that
// proves the F0/F1 fix did not simply widen the refusal. prov_of(MethodCall)
// adopted the receiver's provenance UNCONDITIONALLY once the result carried
// borrows, so an unrelated VALUE-LOCAL receiver made the result look local:
// this program refused while its FREE-FN spelling (identical semantics —
// pass/bc_d1r3_f0_param_root_admits) admitted. Elision cannot separate them:
// `&self -> B` ties the result to self by the declared contract even when the
// body returns its ARGUMENT. The callee's borrow-flow summary can, and does
// (`Id::thru` → result<-{b}, not {self}), so the receiver now contributes only
// what it actually reaches the result with — like any other operand.
// The value-local fallback (Rc::deref's `h.deref()`) is kept, gated on the
// receiver actually being a source.
// KEEP-RED NEIGHBOURS — this admit is an INVERTED pin (it proves a program the
// checker used to refuse now compiles AND runs), so it is only worth anything
// while the refusals at the same site stay red. Verified red alongside it:
// fail/bc_d1r3_f1_method_arg_return (same `i.thru(c.mk())` call, argument
// rooted at a fn-LOCAL), fail/bc_d1r3_f0_call_byval_return (the free-fn
// spelling), fail/bc_d1_holder_chain_held and fail/bc_d1_byvalue_fn_held
// (round-1 by-value hops). Its own admit sibling is
// pass/bc_d1r3_f1_method_arg_admits, which moves the receiver to a &-param and
// keeps everything else identical.
package bc_d1r3_f2_recv_local_admits;
// ⚠ EVERY REGION IS NAMED, and `Id::thru` is why: its result is the ARGUMENT,
// not the receiver, and with `B`'s region elided Rust's elision rule 3 ties it
// to `&self` — so the value would borrow the fn-local `i` and the caller is
// error[E0515]. Decision 2026-09-21 (Victor): Logos follows Rust; a struct with
// a borrowed field names its region and a method says which one it returns.
#[borrow_carrying]
struct B<'a> { p: &'a i64 }
struct C { v: i64 }
impl C {
    fn mk<'a>(self: &'a C) -> B<'a> { return B { p: &self.v }; }
}
struct Id { z: i64 }
impl Id { fn thru<'a>(self: &Id, b: B<'a>) -> B<'a> { return b; } }
fn thru<'a>(c: &'a C) -> B<'a> { let i: Id = Id { z: 0i64 }; return i.thru(c.mk()); }
fn main() -> i32 {
    let c: C = C { v: 1i64 };
    let b: B = thru(&c);
    if *b.p != 1i64 { return 1; }
    return 0;
}
